    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay brings the lens of Marxist feminism to bear on the debate around the extent to which sex work should be decriminalized, to offer a fresh take. According to this framework, the prostitute can be both a “worker” and a victim, just like every other laborer in the capitalist system. Until that hegemonic system gives way to a more equitable one, Ho Kyung argues that we should treat prostitution as any other form of waged labor—recognizing its harmfulness, but neither burdening it with punitive regulations nor singling it for special opprobrium.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
